"RealCLI: Toward a Unified Evaluation ofHuman–Agent Collaboration for CLI Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22507v1)\nciteturn41208view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41196view2\",\"lineno\":130}); Total lines: 550\nL87: Specifically, Understandability concerns whether agents make plans, states, and outcomes visible to users; Steerability concerns whether users can intervene and redirect execution; Observation and Recovery examines whether agents verify feedback and recover from failures; Trust Calibration concerns whether agents faithfully communicate their capability boundaries and potential risks; and Safety and Risk Awareness examines whether agents preserve user control before destructive, sensitive, or unauthorized operations.\nL88: To ground these dimensions in real-world CLI usage, we analyze recurring collaboration needs and failure patterns in anonymized interaction logs, distilling 22 sub-dimensions that are operationalized through 51 interaction scenario templates and six trajectory-level checks. Each scenario specifies the behavior to be evaluated and the evidence required, making collaboration quality observable and auditable.\nL89: We further introduce RealCLI, a paired benchmark that evaluates both task execution and collaboration quality on realistic CLI tasks. RealCLI contains 101 tasks spanning 10 CLI categories and 52 subdomains, from local operations to complex workflows coordinating multiple constraints and deliverables. Each task has two modes sharing the task goal, environment, and verifier: RealCLI-Exec gives the complete specification upfront, while RealCLI-Collab introduces a scripted user during execution.\nL90: In RealCLI-Collab, information slices release the specification progressively, and interaction probes trigger clarification, correction, constraint-revision, and authorization scenarios according to execution states. Both modes judge task outcomes from terminal environment states; RealCLI-Collab additionally scores five-dimensional collaboration behaviors from interaction traces and environment evidence.\nL91: This pairing enables direct comparison between autonomous and collaborative execution, revealing not only how user participation changes task outcomes, but also when task effectiveness and collaboration quality align or diverge.\nL92: We systematically evaluate 33 configurations composed of 11 frontier models and three mainstream agent harnesses. Our main findings are as follows. First, RealCLI poses a substantial challenge to frontier agents. The best configuration attains an average score of only 52.7%, and task effectiveness diverges sharply from collaboration quality, as high task scores do not imply high collaboration quality. Second, user interaction is not a reliable gain.\nL93: Overall pass rates remain essentially unchanged, benefits vary by task type and model, and the additional execution effort does not consistently convert into task progress. Third, collaboration ability is consistently imbalanced across the five dimensions.\nL94: Agents perform relatively well on understandability and on observation and recovery, but remain weak on steerability, trust calibration, and safety and risk awareness, and models with identical task success rates can exhibit sharply different collaboration profiles. These results suggest that the next advance of CLI agents depends not only on stronger autonomous execution but also on understandable, steerable, and trustworthy collaboration.\nL95: Looking ahead, RealCLI provides a reproducible foundation for measuring and improving this capability.\nL96: Table 1: Comparison of RealCLI with representative CLI-agent benchmarks across environment-level verification, runtime user participation, paired evaluation, and five collaboration dimensions. Marks reflect each benchmark’s stated evaluation scope. Ver.: environment-level verification. User: runtime user participation. Pair: paired evaluation of autonomous and user-interactive modes on matched task instances. Und: understandability. Ste: steerability. Obs: observation and recovery. Tru: trust calibration.\nL97: Saf: safety boundary management. Legend: ✓ explicitly evaluated, (✓) partially or secondarily evaluated, and ✗ not evaluated.\nL98: Benchmark  | Ver.  | User  | Pair  | Und  | Ste  | Obs  | Tru  | Saf\nL99: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL100: SWE-bench (cite56†Jimenez et al., 2023 )  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗\nL101: Ambig-SWE (cite65†Vijayvargiya et al., 2025 )  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✗  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗\nL102: Terminal-Bench (cite57†Merrill et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | (✓)  | (✓)  | ✗\nL103: LongCLI-Bench (cite62†Feng et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | (✓)  | (✓)  | ✗  | (✓)  | (✓)  | ✗  | ✗\nL104: Dialogue SWE-Bench (cite70†King and Flanigan, 2026 )  | ✓  | ✓  | ✗  | (✓)  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗\nL105: SWE-Together (cite64†Wu et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | (✓)  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗\nL106: SWE-INTERACT (cite66†Raghavendra et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✗  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗\nL107: UnderSpecBench (cite67†Ji et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | (✓)  | ✗  | ✗  | ✓\nL108: DeepSWE (cite61†Huang et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | (✓)  | ✗  | ✗\nL109: Long-Horizon-Terminal-Bench (cite71†Li et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗  | (✓)  | (✓)  | ✗\nL110: ICAE-Bench (cite72†Peng et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✓  | (✓)  | ✗  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗  | ✗\nL111: SWE-Touch (cite73†Tan et al., 2026 )  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✗  | (✓)  | ✓  | ✗  | ✗\nL112: RealCLI (ours)  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓\nL113: ## 2 RealCLI Benchmark\nL114: \nL115: RealCLI pairs autonomous execution with user collaboration on the same CLI tasks and environments. Both modes share task goals and acceptance criteria. COLLAB adds reproducible, scripted interactions and evaluates five-dimensional collaboration quality alongside task outcomes.\nL116: ### 2.1 Task Formulation\nL117: \nL118: We formalize each RealCLI task as the tuple\nL119: \nL120:  | $$x=(\\mathcal{E},s_{0},\\omega,V).$$  |  | (1)\nL121: Here, $\\mathcal{E}$ is the CLI execution environment, $s_{0}$ is its initial state, $\\omega$ is the complete task specification, and $V$ is the outcome verifier. A run produces trace $\\tau=(o_{0},a_{0},\\ldots,a_{T-1},o_{T})$, with action $a_{t}$ and observation $o_{t}$ at step $t$. RealCLI evaluates the same task $x$ under two modes, EXEC and COLLAB, which differ in their available actions and observations.\nL122: Let $\\mathcal{A}_{E}$ and $\\mathcal{A}_{C}$ denote environment and communication actions, and let $\\mathcal{O}_{E}$ and $\\mathcal{O}_{U}$ denote environment feedback and user messages. For mode $m\\in\\{\\mathrm{EXEC},\\mathrm{COLLAB}\\}$, the action and observation spaces are\nL123:  | $$(\\mathcal{A}_{m},\\mathcal{O}_{m})=\\begin{cases}(\\mathcal{A}_{E},\\mathcal{O}_{E}),&m=\\mathrm{EXEC},\\\\\nL124: (\\mathcal{A}_{E}\\cup\\mathcal{A}_{C},\\,\\mathcal{O}_{E}\\cup\\mathcal{O}_{U}),&m=\\mathrm{COLLAB}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)\nL125: In EXEC, the initial observation $o_{0}^{\\mathrm{EXEC}}$ presents the complete specification $\\omega$, after which observations come only from the environment. COLLAB instead begins with an initial request and progressively introduces the remaining task information through user messages, while allowing the agent to request clarification or authorization.\nL126: Both modes use the same verifier $V$ to evaluate task outcomes from the terminal state $s_{T}$, whereas COLLAB additionally uses the complete interaction trace to assess collaboration quality.\nL127: ### 2.2 Five-Dimensional Collaboration Framework\nL128: RealCLI characterizes the quality of CLI agents’ behavior under user collaboration along five dimensions, defined in Table cite74†2 . Sub-dimensions refine the specific behaviors within each dimension, and scenario templates ground them in concrete CLI task situations.\nL129: For example, one scenario has the user revise an existing requirement mid-execution, testing whether the agent adjusts subsequent work accordingly; another asks the agent to bundle sensitive files under the pretext of daily work habits, testing whether it recognizes the potential leak. See Section cite12†3 for framework construction and Appendix cite24†B for all subdimensions and scenario templates.\nL130: Table 2: Overview of collaboration dimensions (Sub.: subdimensions, Tpl.: scenario templates).\nL131: Dimension  | Evaluation focus  | Sub. / Tpl.\nL132: --- | --- | ---\nL133: U nderstandability (U)  | Legibility of plans, execution state, and outcomes  | 4 / 12\nL134: S teerability (S)  | User intervention, redirection, and constraint persistence  | 5 / 11\nL135: O bservation & R ecovery (OR)  | State verification and recovery from failures  | 3 / 9\nL136: T rust C alibration (TC)  | Accurate disclosure of risks, limits, and task status  | 5 / 12\nL137: S afety & R isk Awareness (SR)  | User control over high-impact or unauthorized actions  | 5 / 13\nL138: ### 2.3 Script-Driven User Simulation\nL139: \nL140: RealCLI uses a task-level user script to control how task information enters the interaction and when the user participates. Its core consists of information slices and interaction probes: the former governs the progressive release of task information, while the latter introduces collaboration situations during execution. For task $x$, the simulator at the $k$-th user interaction is defined as\nL141:  | $$\\mathcal{U}_{x}^{k}=\\left(c_{x},\\mathcal{I}_{x},\\mathcal{P}_{x},z_{k}\\right).$$  |  | (3)\nL142: \nL143: where $c_{x}$ specifies the user role and the opening and closing of the interaction, $\\mathcal{I}_{x}$ and $\\mathcal{P}_{x}$ denote the information slices and interaction probes, and $z_{k}$ denotes the current runtime state.\nL144: Information Slices. $\\mathcal{I}_{x}$ decomposes the complete task specification $\\omega$ into slices released progressively during execution. Slices are either provided in response to agent inquiries or proactively supplied by the simulator.\nL145: Interaction Probes. $\\mathcal{P}_{x}$ instantiates the scenario templates of the five-dimensional collaboration framework into probes tied to the current task. Each probe contains a scripted user intervention with its trigger condition, expected behavior, and evidence requirements. The trigger condition determines when the intervention enters the execution trajectory, and the evidence requirements specify how the agent’s response is verified.\nL146: At the $k$-th interaction, the simulator selects the applicable information slices and probes based on the preceding execution trajectory and state $z_{k}$, generates the user message, and updates the state. In this way, the timing of user interventions varies with the execution trajectory, while task semantics, key messages, and evaluation criteria remain fixed.\nL147: ### 2.4 Evaluation Metrics\nL148: \nL149: The evaluation framework of REALCLI consists of two independent axes: Task Success and Collaboration Quality. To measure the agent’s ability to balance both, we further introduce the Joint Collaboration Score.\nL150: Task Success. Task success measures whether the agent’s execution result satisfies the task goal and acceptance criteria. Let $\\mathcal{X}$ denote the evaluation task set. For task $x$, the task verifier $V_{x}$ produces a binary outcome from the terminal environment state $s_{T}^{m}$ under mode $m$. The average task reward under mode $m$ is defined as\nL151: \nL152:  | $$R_{m}=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{X}|}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{X}}V_{x}(s_{T}^{m}),\\qquad V_{x}(s_{T}^{m})\\in\\{0,1\\}.$$  |  | (4)\nL153: $R_{\\mathrm{EXEC}}$ and $R_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$ denote the average task scores under EXEC and COLLAB, respectively.\nL154: \nL155: Collaboration Quality. To accurately measure the agent’s collaboration proficiency during dynamic interactions, we construct a four-level bottom-up aggregation logic. This logic aggregates micro-behavioral checks into a macro-level composite capability score ($S_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$):\nL156: \nL157:   * •\nL158: Level 1: Task-Level Scenario Perfection. For a specific collaboration scenario $q$ triggered in task $t$, the agent must satisfy all applicable checklist items to receive a score of 1 (complete pass). Any omission or violation results in a score of 0. This strict all-or-nothing criterion ensures the flawless execution of individual collaborative behaviors.\nL159: \nL160:   * •\nL161: Level 2: Cross-Task Scenario Consistency. We evaluate the average pass rate of a scenario $q$ across the entire test set (i.e., all tasks where $q$ is triggered). To ensure the agent exhibits stable and reliable collaboration, we enforce a strict 75% threshold: the final score $\\hat{r}_{q}$ for the scenario is 1 (pass) if and only if its overall pass rate is $\\geq 0.75$; otherwise, it is 0.\nL162: \nL163:  | $$\\hat{r}_{q}=\\mathbb{I}\\left(\\text{pass rate of }q\\geq 0.75\\right)$$  |  | (5)\nL164: \nL165:   * •\nL166: Level 3: Subdimension & Dimension Score. After determining the binary pass status of each scenario, we aggregate the scores upwards using equal-weight averaging:\nL167: \nL168:     1. 1.\nL169: \nL170: Subdimension Score ($s_{u}$): The arithmetic mean of the scenario scores ($\\hat{r}_{q}$) within the subdimension $u$.\nL171: \nL172:     2. 2.\nL173: Dimension Score ($s_{d}$): The score for each of the five core dimensions (Understandability $U$, Steerability $S$, Observation and Recovery $OR$, Trust Calibration $TC$, and Safety and Risk Awareness $SR$) is the arithmetic mean of its constituent subdimension scores ($s_{u}$).\nL174: \nL175:   * •\nL176: \nL177: Level 4: Composite Collaboration Score ($S_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$). Finally, the composite collaboration score, which represents the global interaction quality, is the equal-weight average of the five core dimension scores:\nL178:  | $$S_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}=\\frac{1}{5}(s_{U}+s_{S}+s_{OR}+s_{TC}+s_{SR})$$  |  | (6)\nL179: Joint Collaboration Score. In real-world complex workflows, an agent that silently completes tasks while ignoring safety warnings or user feedback is impractical. To quantify the agent’s comprehensive ability to simultaneously complete tasks and maintain high-quality collaboration, we define the Joint Collaboration Score ($J_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$) as the direct product of the collaborative task success rate and the composite collaboration score:\nL180:  | $$J_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}=R_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}\\times S_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}\\in[0,1]$$  |  | (7)\nL181: \nL182: This multiplicative formulation establishes a stringent penalty mechanism. Specifically, a high task success rate ($R_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$) cannot compensate for fundamental deficiencies in collaborative capabilities ($S_{\\mathrm{COLLAB}}$). Consequently, any shortcomings in critical areas, including communication, steerability, and safety, will proportionately degrade the overall joint performance metric.\nL183: cite75†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Construction pipeline of RealCLI.\nL184: ## 3 Benchmark Construction\nL185: \nL186: As shown in Figure cite76†2 , RealCLI is constructed through two sequential pipelines: base instance synthesis from real CLI logs and interactive instance synthesis from validated base instances.\nL187: Base Instance Synthesis. We derive offline-reproducible task patterns from real CLI logs. Log Compression condenses raw traces into task goals, environment states, constraints, and failure signals. During Skill-Oriented Instance Construction, this evidence is transformed into reusable skills capturing operational patterns, environment dependencies, and corrective actions. These skills guide the synthesis of task instructions, containerized environments, reference solutions, and verifiers.\nL188: Quality Checks inspect files and container configurations, specification–test alignment, and verify that reference solutions pass while no-op baselines fail. Candidates then undergo multi-agent, multi-run rollouts. Agent Rollout & Diagnosis distinguishes task issues from infrastructure issues, routing task issues back to instance repair while retrying infrastructure issues. Difficulty Profiling classifies the resulting candidates as easy, medium, or hard.\nL189: Medium and hard candidates enter the validated base-instance pool, whereas easy candidates are augmented with additional skills and re-enter instance construction and validation.\nL190: Interactive Instance Synthesis. We augment each validated base instance with an interaction layer supporting gradual information disclosure and scenario-driven probes across five stages.\nL191: \nL192: (1) Scenario Assignment. For each base task, we assign six distinct collaboration scenarios based on task semantics, execution characteristics, and predefined templates, while balancing coverage across task types and the five-dimensional collaboration framework.\nL193: (2) Interactive Script Authoring. This phase produces an interaction script comprising an opening message, information slices, scenario probes, and checklist items. The opening message provides partial information, while information slices progressively disclose requirements and context. Each probe specifies a target collaboration behavior, expected evidence, and linked checklist items.\nL194: We render each probe as a user message presented to an uninformed agent, revising any probes that prematurely leak their evaluation intent.\nL195: (3) Script Validation. Static Checks verify script structure, file references, and alignment among scenarios, probes, and checklist items. Semantic Review assesses task–scenario fit, dialogue naturalness, and evidence traceability. Failed scripts return to authoring for revision.\nL196: (4) End-to-End Pilot. A target agent completes the task alongside a user simulator. Judge Review examines the resulting trajectory scenario by scenario to confirm that each intended collaboration capability is successfully elicited and measurable.\nL197: (5) Final Curation. Candidates passing the pilot are submitted to human expert review and difficulty filtering. Instances that successfully pass final curation retain their base task artifacts, append the specific files required for user interaction, and enter the delivery pool.\nL198: \nL199: Figure cite77†3 summarizes RealCLI’s statistics across 101 tasks, spanning 10 top-level categories and 52 subdomains, 51 collaboration scenario templates, and 1,658 evaluation checklist items.\nL200: Figure 3: Statistics dashboard of RealCLI. Fig. (a) shows the task categories and their subdomains. Fig. (b), (c) show the distributions of instruction length and of itemized requirements per task, and Fig. (d) the number of distinct file paths cited per instruction. Fig. (e) shows how the task verifiers are realized. Fig. (f) shows the task coverage of each subdimension of our collaboration taxonomy, and Fig. (g) the distribution of tasks over the scenarios.\nL201: Fig. (h) shows the evidence source of the checklist items, and Fig. (i) the per-task spread of the four interaction item kinds.\nL202: ## 4 EXPERIMENTS\nL203: ### 4.1 experimental Setup\nL204: Table 3: RealCLI main results on the held-out split across three agent scaffolds. Subscripts on $R_{collab}$ and $J_{collab}$ indicate performance changes relative to the $R_{exec}$ baseline (green positive, red negative). Bold and underline denote the highest and second-highest scores, respectively.\nL205: Model  | Claude Code  | Terminus-2  | Codex  | Avg.\nL206:  | $R_{\\mathrm{exec}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $J_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{exec}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $J_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{exec}}$  | $R_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  | $J_{\\mathrm{collab}}$  |\nL207: Closed-source models\nL208: ---\nL209: Claude Opus 5  | 58.2  | 58.4_{+0.2}  | 27.4_{-30.8}  | 65.4  | 62.4_{-3.